\f1\fs22 Since Eloise cut short her interview with Sarah before the attack on the mansion and the subsequent escape to Eloise\'92s compound in the mountains, Hayley\'92s questions for Sarah had multiplied. And she was impatient for answers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the time Sarah was out chatting with Eloise, Hayley showered, made a pot of camomile tea, and settled into a luxuriously accommodating sofa in the front room of the townhouse. Hayley sat facing set of French doors which afforded a view of manicured native shrubs and flowers, and glimpses of the street beyond. The morning was pristine and crisp which chilled and intensified the colours of the still garden. As Hayley sipped her tea and meditated on the view outside the windows, a plan for interviewing Sarah crystallised. With confidence growing, she relaxed and  allowed her mind to wander to other things.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To other people.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy. Hayley\'92s eyes glinted as she applied her smile to her cup and savoured the last of the tea. She  crossed her legs and felt a frisson of delight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy: so inexplicable. While Hayley brewed the tea earlier, she could observe Kathy in the back yard rolling on her back on the grass as she threw her hands in the air in some ecstatic ritual of devotion to the sky. 
\f2\i Shamanic
\f1\i0 , Hayley thought. Doctor Lanning was some kind of witch, living in the two worlds of science and atavistic tradition.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley liked that. She wished she could be as free as Kathy appeared to be. 
\f2\i Well that\'92s bull-tax
\f1\i0 , she immediately self edited. 
\f2\i You are your ability to restore order, 
\f1\i0 her inner critic reminded her. And true that\'92s what she sought to do all her life; in the chaotic jeopardy of her childhood home, and in every relationship since. It was why she joined the police force, and actively resisted the axiomatic corruption in it. In spite of all that, Hayley toyed with the idea of incorporating a little more Kathy-ness in her life.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A movement of colours through the glimpses of the street brought Hayley out of reverie. Steps on the gravel path. The front door clicked open and shortly shut with gentler percussion. The hall closet made similar sounds too\'97Sarah hanging up the coat she borrowed to go outside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh hi!\'92 Sarah said, surprised to see Hayley in the front room. \'91Everything good?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley said nothing for a few moments. \'91I was wondering if we could pick up on our conversation that Eloise interrupted.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stiffened. \'91You mean your interrogation.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley let Sarah take breath, then said, \'91I don\'92t even know whether this is a sanctuary here or a prison. Wouldn\'92t you like to pool information? We can be allies. We can just have an amicable chat.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah exhaled and her shoulders regained some of the suppleness. \'91OK. Shall I get Kathy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In spite of the sofa being designed for curling up in, Hayley tried sitting with a straighter back. \'91Just us for now, OK?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah noticed the way Hayley held her tea cup; cradling in one hand while gripping it tighter in the other, like she was choking it. \'91What are you drinking?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, camomile tea. The pot\'92s in the kitchen. You may be able to still get a fresh brew if you add more water.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sure. Like some more?\'92 Sarah extended her hand to accept Hayley\'92s cup.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Please.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Sarah returned and settled on the sofa alongside her, Hayley allowed some drawn-out moments while each tasted and reflected on the brew in the cups. \'91You and Tony Supaporn: you seem like an unlikely match,\'92 she said without taking her eyes off her tea.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh we\'92re not,\'92 Sarah said congruently, and with some amusement. \'91We\'92re not any kind of match.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you often buy underwear for people you are not in relationship with?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah laughed and had to put her cup on the table. \'91Oh I was intending to tease him with them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How so?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The first time I met him, he just look so pathetic, and a little bit adorable \'85 he was trying to hide a rip in his overalls from me, which was hard because the lurid pink of his underwear couldn\'92t have been brighter than if he had a lighthouse in his pants.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you often encounter men who have lighthouses in their pants?\'92 Hayley smiled over her tea cup, and slid a quick glance Sarah\'92s way. \'91When was that, the first time you met?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It was the first day at my new job \'85 awful, awful \'85 one of the execs there had 
\f2\i retired
\f1\i0  as they like to say \'85 shot himself in the head with a shotgun.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92re referring to Travis Teh?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, I am.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The first time you met Tony Supaporn was the day Teh died?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, he\'92d come to clean the office.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And you\'92d never met before \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No. Why?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s strange.\'92 Hayley kept casting her foveal vision towards the flowers in the garden. Though it sacrificed seeing Sarah\'92s micro-movements in detail\'97the lips not quite tightening, the cheeks almost flushing, the arrested gulp, the pupils dilating by the merest amount\'97what Hayley saw through the corner of her eye was a gestalt essence, a more faithful abstraction that flushed with truth. 
\f2\i Here it comes
\f1\i0 , she thought, and sipped tea.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92re about to ask why I met up with Tony later that day,\'92 Sarah said, deciding to get ahead of the inconsistency.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91At the 
\f2\i Stick-It Inn
\f1\i0 ,\'92  Hayley laid our for Sarah.\
